      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-3">MilesWeb VPS Review 2026: Managed Linux KVM from $69.99/mo</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 text-base">Last updated: August 20, 2026 · 9 min read</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-gray-300 leading-relaxed space-y-5">

      <p>Most of the budget-VPS conversation on this site revolves around unmanaged KVM boxes at rock-bottom prices — and rightly so, since those deliver the best compute per dollar when you are comfortable running your own server. But there is a large audience that wants the opposite trade: a fully managed Linux VPS with cPanel, human support, and the hosting company handling updates and security patching. MilesWeb has served that segment since 2009 and now sells managed and unmanaged Linux KVM VPS across <strong>32 global data centers</strong>, with managed plans starting at <strong>$69.99/month</strong>. We pulled the live pricing and feature set from the milesweb.com VPS page and compared it against our existing <a href="/vps" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">VPSTier-covered lineup</a> to see where it fits.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">MilesWeb at a Glance</h2>
      <p>MilesWeb is one of the larger independently-owned managed hosting brands founded outside the US — established in 2009 with headquarters in both the UK and India — and has expanded to serve customers globally. Its VPS line is built on KVM virtualization with NVMe SSD storage and a 1 Gbps network uplink on every plan. The headline difference from the budget hosts we usually review is the <strong>managed service layer</strong>: on managed plans, the MilesWeb team handles OS updates, security patching, firewall configuration, and proactive monitoring, and the vendor <a href="https://www.milesweb.com/vps-hosting/" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">advertises free end-to-end migration</a> for files, databases, email, and DNS.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Managed vs. Unmanaged — the key decision</h2>
      <p>Before the pricing, it is worth deciding which type you actually need, because the two are very different products. MilesWeb advertises both, so the choice is yours:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>Managed Linux VPS:</strong> the MilesWeb team handles the entire server layer — updates, security patching, and configuration. You get a control panel (cPanel, CyberPanel, Webuzo, Plesk, or DirectAdmin) and can hand off tasks like installing a panel or hardening the firewall. Best for small businesses, agencies, and non-technical site owners.</li>
        <li><strong>Unmanaged Linux VPS:</strong> full root access at a lower price, but basic Linux familiarity is expected because you run and secure the server yourself. Best for developers and sysadmins who want a plain KVM box without paying a management premium.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Both share the same underlying KVM + NVMe + 1 Gbps infrastructure. The premium you pay for the managed tier is effectively the human support and the proactive patching/monitoring layer, not faster hardware.</p>

